from pathlib import Path
import sys
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'authoring'))
from engine import Author
A=Author('5');add=A.add
add('2.3.1-01','Position describes where an object is relative to a chosen reference point.','A coordinate and a positive direction make one-dimensional positions unambiguous.',
'A mark on a straight track can be chosen as x = 0, with east defined as positive. A trolley at x = 2 m is then two metres east of that mark. Choosing a different reference changes the coordinate assigned to the same physical place. It does not by itself move the trolley. State the reference and direction before comparing positions or using their changes to calculate velocity. In two or three dimensions, more than one coordinate is needed to locate an object.',
'State what must accompany a one-dimensional position coordinate so that its meaning is unambiguous.','A reference origin and a specified positive direction are needed.')
add('2.3.1-02','A position can be positive, zero or negative relative to a chosen origin.','A negative position identifies one side of the origin; it does not by itself say how the object is moving.',
'With east positive, x = −4 m locates an object four metres west of the origin. It could be stationary, travelling east or travelling west at that instant. Position and velocity are different quantities. If the origin is moved, the coordinate changes even though the object may stay still. To find displacement, subtract initial position from final position using the same origin and sign convention for both readings.',
'With east chosen positive, state the meaning of x = −3 m.','The object is 3 m west of the chosen origin.')
add('2.3.1-03','Distance is the length of the path travelled.','It is a scalar and does not encode the direction between the starting and finishing positions.',
'Walking 7 m east and then 2 m west gives a travelled distance of 9 m. The return section adds to the path length even though it partly reverses the earlier movement. A route with several bends can have a long distance while the finishing position is close to the start. Keep total path length separate from a coordinate measured from an origin. Units of metres do not distinguish them by themselves; the physical definition does.',
'Define distance travelled.','Distance is the length of the path followed by the object.')
add('2.3.1-04','Displacement is the directed change in position from start to finish.','Its magnitude is the straight-line separation of those positions, with a direction from initial to final position.',
'For motion on a straight east-positive line, a change from x = −2 m to x = +5 m gives displacement +7 m. An object that returns to its starting point has zero net displacement even if it has travelled a substantial distance. Draw a start-to-finish arrow to separate net displacement from the intermediate route. A negative component is a direction relative to the stated convention, not a negative path length.',
'Define displacement.','Displacement is the directed change from the initial position to the final position.')
add('2.3.2-01','Speed is distance travelled per elapsed time.','Instantaneous speed describes how rapidly distance is changing at a particular instant.',
'A vehicle speedometer indicates an instantaneous speed rather than necessarily the average over a whole journey. Average speed uses the total path length and the complete elapsed time being considered. A speed of 6 m/s means six metres of path are covered per second if that speed remains steady. Speed carries no direction, so two objects moving oppositely can have equal speeds. Use a velocity when the direction matters.',
'Define speed.','Speed is the rate of change of distance.')
add('2.3.2-02','Instantaneous velocity is the rate of change of displacement at an instant.','It includes direction as well as the magnitude of motion.',
'With east positive, velocities +4 m/s and −4 m/s have the same speed but opposite directions. A velocity can change because the speed changes, the direction changes or both change. At this level a straight-line sign convention represents direction clearly. Do not infer direction from a speed-time graph alone unless the motion’s direction is supplied. The unit m/s is shared with speed, so the definition and sign convention must distinguish the two quantities.',
'Define instantaneous velocity.','It is the rate of change of displacement at a particular instant, including direction.')
add('2.3.2-03','Average velocity is total displacement divided by total elapsed time.','It can be zero for a return journey whose average speed is not zero.',
'If a learner travels 12 m away and then returns to the start in 8 s, the net displacement is zero and the average velocity is zero. The path distance is 24 m, so the average speed is 3 m/s. Stops count in the elapsed time if the requested average covers the full journey. A nonzero average velocity must be given with a direction or a signed component under a stated convention.',
'Define average velocity.','Average velocity is net displacement divided by the total elapsed time.')
add('2.3.2-04','Positive and negative velocities identify opposite directions under the chosen sign convention.','The sign of velocity is independent of whether the speed is increasing or decreasing.',
'A trolley moving west at a steady 2 m/s has velocity −2 m/s if east is positive. Its negative sign does not mean it is slowing. If it changes from −2 to −5 m/s, its speed grows from 2 to 5 m/s even though the signed velocity decreases. State the chosen positive direction before describing the change. On a position-time graph, a negative gradient indicates motion in the negative coordinate direction.',
'Explain why a trolley moving at a constant −2 m/s is not necessarily decelerating.','The negative sign gives its direction relative to the chosen positive direction.~Its speed stays 2 m/s, so its velocity is constant and its acceleration is zero.')
add('2.3.2-05','Average speed is total distance divided by total elapsed time.','An average over unequal-duration stages cannot generally be found by an unweighted mean of their speeds.',
'A journey covers 24 m in 6 s and a further 12 m in 6 s. Total distance is 36 m and total time is 12 s, giving 3 m/s. If either stage included a stop, include that stopped interval when calculating a full-journey average. Identify the boundary of the journey before adding distances and times. A simple average of two stage speeds is valid for equal durations, but it should not replace the total-distance method when durations differ.',
'Calculate the whole-journey average speed for 30 m travelled in 5 s followed by 18 m in 7 s.','Average speed = total distance / total time = 48/12.~The average is 4.0 m/s.')
add('2.3.2-06','The gradient of a distance-time graph represents speed.','A horizontal section means that travelled distance is not increasing during that interval.',
'Plot accumulated path distance vertically and elapsed time horizontally. A straight rising segment indicates steady speed; a steeper segment on the same scales indicates a higher speed. A horizontal part indicates rest over the interval. If speed changes smoothly, the graph bends and a local gradient describes the instantaneous speed. A path-distance graph does not decrease when an object turns back: accumulated distance continues to grow. A signed position-time graph can decrease, because its vertical quantity is different.',
'Draw a distance-time graph for an object travelling 6 m in the first 3 s at steady speed, then resting for 2 s.','Draw a straight rise from (0 s, 0 m) to (3 s, 6 m).~Continue horizontally to (5 s, 6 m), with labelled axes.')
add('2.3.2-07','The gradient of a speed-time graph represents the rate of speed change during straight-line motion of unchanged direction.','Area between the speed-time line and the time axis gives travelled distance.',
'A horizontal line at a positive speed represents steady motion, not rest. A line on the time axis represents zero speed. A straight sloping segment gives uniform speed change, while a curved segment gives a changing rate. Read the vertical quantity before interpreting the shape. For motion whose direction changes, use signed velocity information to determine acceleration direction; a speed graph alone omits that direction. Distance can be found by dividing the area into simple rectangles, triangles or trapezia.',
'Draw a speed-time graph for uniform acceleration from 2 m/s at time zero to 8 m/s at 3 s.','Label time horizontally and speed vertically.~Draw the straight segment joining (0 s, 2 m/s) to (3 s, 8 m/s).')
add('2.3.2-08','Graph shape must be interpreted using the quantity on the vertical axis.','Rest, steady motion and changing motion can look different on distance-time and speed-time graphs.',
'A horizontal distance-time line means that the accumulated distance does not grow, so the object is at rest. A horizontal speed-time line at 4 m/s describes an object continuing to travel at that speed. A straight rising distance-time line represents steady speed, while a straight rising speed-time line represents increasing speed. State the axis labels before describing any segment. If a line bends, examine how its gradient changes rather than attaching one memorised motion label to every curved line.',
'Draw speed-time lines for an object at rest and an object moving steadily at 4 m/s over 3 s. Label the two cases.','Rest is the horizontal line at zero speed.~Steady motion is a horizontal line at 4 m/s, on labelled time and speed axes.')
add('2.3.2-09','Speed describes the rate of distance travelled without direction.','Velocity describes the rate of displacement change and includes direction.',
'An object moving around a bend at steady speed can still have changing velocity because its direction changes. For straight-line examples, two objects at 5 m/s in opposite directions have equal speeds but different velocities. Whole-journey averages also differ when path distance is greater than the net displacement. A numerical answer expressed in m/s is incomplete as a velocity specification unless the direction is given or implied by a clear sign convention.',
'Explain the difference between equal speeds and equal velocities for two vehicles on the same straight road.','Equal speeds require equal magnitudes.~Equal velocities require that magnitude and direction match.')
add('2.3.3-01','Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity.','It has direction and a unit of metres per second per second, written m/s² or m s⁻².',
'If velocity changes by 6 m/s during 3 s, the average acceleration is 2 m/s² in the direction of that velocity change. For a constant acceleration the same change occurs during each equal time interval. Acceleration need not increase speed: it can oppose the current velocity and reduce the speed. A direction change can also be acceleration even when the speed stays the same. Straight-line examples require a stated positive direction to interpret the signs.',
'Define acceleration.','Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity.')
add('2.3.3-02','The sign of an acceleration component gives its direction under the chosen coordinate convention.','Whether speed increases depends on how the acceleration direction compares with the velocity direction.',
'For east-positive motion, changing from +7 to +3 m/s gives negative acceleration and a speed decrease. Changing from −3 to −7 m/s also gives negative acceleration, but the speed increases. The word negative therefore cannot be used as a universal synonym for slowing down. Compare initial velocity, final velocity and the chosen positive direction. If velocity and acceleration are oppositely directed, speed decreases while that direction relationship holds.',
'Explain why a velocity change from −3 m/s to −7 m/s represents increasing speed despite a negative acceleration.','The magnitude of velocity increases from 3 to 7 m/s.~The negative acceleration is directed the same way as the negative velocity, so it increases that speed.')
add('2.3.3-03','Constant acceleration gives equal velocity changes during equal time intervals.','For uniform straight-line acceleration, a = (v − u)/t.',
'A model vehicle changing from 1 m/s to 5 m/s in 2 s has acceleration 2 m/s². The velocity-time plot is straight because its gradient stays constant. Identify both ends of the time interval; using the final velocity alone would be valid only if the initial velocity were zero. Include the sign in the velocity difference. The average acceleration formula still gives an interval average for nonuniform motion, but it does not establish that the acceleration was constant throughout.',
'Calculate the constant acceleration when a straight-line velocity changes from +2 m/s to +11 m/s in 3.0 s.','a = (11 − 2)/3.0.~The acceleration is +3.0 m/s².')
add('2.3.3-04','Nonuniform acceleration means that the rate of velocity change varies during the motion.','A curved velocity-time graph can show a changing gradient and therefore changing acceleration.',
'Velocities of 0, 1, 4 and 9 m/s at one-second intervals have successive increases of 1, 3 and 5 m/s. These changes are not equal, so one constant acceleration does not describe the sequence. A secant gradient between distant points gives an average over that interval, while the curve’s local slope describes the instantaneous change. When sketching such motion, choose a smooth changing gradient rather than a straight line simply because the velocity is increasing.',
'Draw a velocity-time trend through readings 0, 1, 4 and 9 m/s at times 0, 1, 2 and 3 s to show increasing acceleration.','Plot the stated values on labelled axes.~Draw a smooth upward-curving trend whose gradient becomes steeper.')
add('2.3.3-05','Distance travelled is the area beneath a speed-time graph over the chosen interval.','The graph’s area has units of speed multiplied by time, which reduces to a length.',
'For a speed rising uniformly from 2 to 6 m/s during 4 s, the trapezium area is one half of the sum of the parallel speed values multiplied by the time width. The distance is 16 m. The gradient of the same line is instead 1 m/s², which is an acceleration. Splitting the area into a rectangle and a triangle gives the same physical distance. With a signed velocity-time graph, signed area gives displacement; adding magnitudes of the appropriate areas is needed for total path distance.',
'Calculate the distance travelled when speed rises uniformly from 0 to 8 m/s during 5 s.','Distance is the triangular area: ½ × 5 × 8.~The distance is 20 m.')
add('2.3.3-06','Near Earth’s surface, freely falling motion has a gravitational acceleration of approximately 10 m/s² when air resistance is negligible.','The acceleration is directed downward and is not the same quantity as the object’s current speed.',
'A released object begins with an initial speed that may be zero but has a downward acceleration immediately. In the simple model its downward speed then increases by approximately 10 m/s during each second. A thrown object is still accelerated downward while rising, at its highest point and while falling. The sign assigned to this acceleration depends on the chosen positive direction. Air resistance can change the resultant acceleration, so the simple value should not be applied without considering the stated model.',
'State the approximate magnitude and direction of free-fall acceleration near Earth’s surface when air resistance is negligible.','Approximately 10 m/s² downward.')
add('2.3.3-07','Constant-acceleration equations connect u, v, a, t and displacement s for straight-line motion under fixed acceleration.','Use v = u + at, s = ut + ½at², v² = u² + 2as and s = (u + v)t/2 only when their constant-acceleration condition applies.',
'Choose an equation containing the known quantities and the unknown required. To find final velocity from initial velocity, acceleration and time, use v = u + at. To find displacement without a time value, v² = u² + 2as may be appropriate. Use one sign convention for every vector component; displacement can be negative under that convention. An object may reverse direction during an interval, in which case displacement and total path distance differ. Do not replace a changing acceleration with a constant one merely to make an equation convenient.',
'Calculate the final velocity of an object with u = 3 m/s and a constant acceleration of 2 m/s² over 4 s in the same positive direction.','v = u + at = 3 + 2 × 4.~The final velocity is +11 m/s.')
A.misconception('MIS-01','A positive horizontal speed-time segment represents steady motion.','A horizontal distance-time segment represents no additional path distance and therefore rest over the interval.',
'Consider graph A at a constant speed of 3 m/s and graph B at a constant accumulated distance of 12 m. During the next two seconds, A adds 6 m to its path, while B adds none. Both lines are horizontal, but they plot different vertical quantities. The error is treating the shape as a motion label without reading the axis. Use the quantity and its unit to decide whether the line directly states a speed or instead requires a gradient to obtain it.',
'Graph A is horizontal at 3 m/s on speed-time axes. Graph B is horizontal at 12 m on distance-time axes. Explain which object is moving during the shown interval.','A is moving at a steady 3 m/s; its horizontal line states a nonzero speed.~B is at rest because its accumulated distance is unchanged.',guidance=['Correct distinction: A moves and B is at rest (1 mark).','Reason: the graphs plot different quantities; nonzero constant speed differs from unchanged distance (1 mark).'])
A.misconception('MIS-02','Speed-time area gives distance, whereas speed-time gradient gives a rate of speed change.','Their different units distinguish the two operations.',
'A straight line rising from 1 to 5 m/s over 2 s has area 6 m and gradient 2 m/s². The first result combines speed with time; the second divides a speed change by a time change. Calling the slope the distance is not a small numerical slip: it uses the wrong relationship between the plotted quantities. Identify the requested quantity before selecting the graphical operation.',
'A speed-time line rises uniformly from 1 m/s to 5 m/s in 2 s. A learner calls its gradient the travelled distance. Explain the error using the correct distance and gradient.','The gradient is (5 − 1)/2 = 2 m/s², a rate of speed change.~Distance is the trapezium area, ½ × (1 + 5) × 2 = 6 m.',guidance=['Identify the confusion: gradient describes acceleration here, not distance (1 mark).','Give the correct distance from graph area, 6 m, distinguished from the 2 m/s² gradient (1 mark).'])
A.misconception('MIS-03','Whole-journey average speed uses total path distance and total elapsed time.','An arithmetic mean of stage speeds requires equal durations to represent this average.',
'For 2 m/s during 6 s followed by 5 m/s during 2 s, the distances are 12 m and 10 m. The total is 22 m in 8 s, giving 2.75 m/s. The unweighted mean of the two speeds is 3.5 m/s and would give the shorter fast stage too much influence. Multiplying each stage speed by its duration first preserves how far the object actually travelled. Include any rest interval in a complete-journey time.',
'An object moves at 2 m/s for 6 s and 5 m/s for 2 s. A learner averages the speeds to obtain 3.5 m/s. Explain why this is incorrect and give the whole-journey average.','The unequal durations mean an unweighted speed mean is inappropriate.~The total distance is 22 m in 8 s, so the average is 2.75 m/s.',guidance=['Identify the error: the stage durations differ (1 mark).','Correct result from total distance divided by total time: 2.75 m/s (1 mark).'])
A.plot('2.3.2-06',[0,3,5],{'Distance':[0,6,6]},'Time / s','Distance / m','Steady travel followed by rest')
A.plot('2.3.2-07',[0,3],{'Speed':[2,8]},'Time / s','Speed / m s⁻¹','Uniform speed increase')
A.plot('2.3.2-08',[0,1,2,3],{'At rest':[0,0,0,0],'Steady motion':[4,4,4,4]},'Time / s','Speed / m s⁻¹','Two horizontal lines, different speeds')
A.plot('2.3.3-04',[0,1,2,3],{'Velocity':[0,1,4,9]},'Time / s','Velocity / m s⁻¹','A changing gradient')
C=A.choice
C('2.3.1-03','Which quantity is the full length of a travelled path?',('Distance','Distance adds the path lengths.'),('Displacement','This measures the directed start-to-finish change.'),('Velocity','This is a rate, not a path length.'),('Acceleration','This describes velocity change per time.'))
C('2.3.1-02','With east positive, what does position −6 m indicate?',('Six metres west of the origin','The negative coordinate locates the position west of the reference.'),('A speed of 6 m/s west','Position is not a velocity.'),('A negative path distance','Path length is a different quantity.'),('That the object is slowing','A position coordinate does not determine acceleration.'))
C('2.3.2-03','Which expression gives average velocity?',('Net displacement / total time','Both direction and the complete interval matter.'),('Total distance / total time','This is average speed.'),('Velocity change / time','This is average acceleration.'),('Time / displacement','This reverses the physical ratio.'))
C('2.3.2-08','What does a horizontal speed-time line above zero show?',('Steady nonzero speed','The vertical value is a speed that is not changing.'),('Rest','That would require zero speed.'),('Uniform acceleration away from rest','That needs a sloping speed-time line for unchanged direction.'),('A decreasing distance','Accumulated distance is not the vertical variable.'))
C('2.3.3-01','Which unit measures acceleration?',('m/s²','Velocity changes by metres per second during each second.'),('m/s','This is a speed or velocity unit.'),('m²/s','This is not the velocity-change/time dimension.'),('s/m','This is reciprocal speed.'))
C('2.3.3-02','What does negative acceleration alone identify?',('Its direction under the chosen sign convention','The effect on speed also depends on velocity direction.'),('That speed must decrease','Negative velocity can grow in magnitude under negative acceleration.'),('That the object is at a negative position','Position is independent information.'),('That no force acts','A nonzero acceleration requires a resultant force in the simple constant-mass model.'))
C('2.3.3-07','When are the standard equations v = u + at and s = ut + ½at² applicable?',('For the stated straight-line motion with constant acceleration','The acceleration condition is built into them.'),('For any varying acceleration without modification','That violates their condition.'),('Only when the object starts from rest','They allow a nonzero u.'),('Only when all signs are positive','Consistent signed components are permitted.'))
C('2.3.1-04','A learner walks 10 m east then 4 m west. What is the net displacement?',('6 m east','Subtract the opposing displacement components.'),('14 m east','This is the total path length with a direction incorrectly attached.'),('6 m west','This reverses the net direction.'),('0 m','The learner has not returned to the starting point.'))
C('2.3.2-05','An object travels 36 m in 6 s then 12 m in 6 s. What is its average speed?',('4 m/s','The total is 48 m in 12 s.'),('8 m/s','This adds stage speeds instead of averaging the full journey.'),('2 m/s','This uses the second stage alone.'),('6 m/s','This uses the first stage alone.'))
C('2.3.3-03','Velocity changes from 4 m/s to 10 m/s in 3 s. What is the average acceleration?',('2 m/s²','The change is 6 m/s over 3 s.'),('3.33 m/s²','This divides final velocity by time and omits u.'),('18 m/s²','This multiplies the velocity change by time.'),('14 m/s²','This adds the velocities without a time ratio.'))
C('2.3.3-05','Speed is 5 m/s for 4 s. What distance is represented by that speed-time rectangle?',('20 m','Speed multiplied by duration gives the rectangular area.'),('1.25 m','This divides speed by time instead of using area.'),('9 m','This adds unlike quantities.'),('0 m','Zero gradient means constant speed, not zero distance.'))
C('2.3.3-07','From rest, an object has constant acceleration 1.5 m/s² for 4 s. What is its displacement?',('12 m','Use one half times acceleration times time squared.'),('6 m','This is the final speed value with the wrong dimension.'),('24 m','This omits the factor one half.'),('3 m','This does not account for the time-squared factor.'))
P=A.part
P('2.3.1-04','State whether displacement is scalar or vector.','Displacement is a vector.',1,'One mark for vector.')
P('2.3.3-01','State the SI unit of acceleration.','m/s²',1,'One mark for a valid acceleration unit.')
P('2.3.2-08','Explain why a horizontal distance-time line indicates rest whereas a horizontal speed-time line at 2 m/s does not.','The first has no increase in path distance. The second directly states a constant nonzero speed, so the object continues moving.',2,'One mark for unchanged distance; one for the nonzero speed distinction.')
P('2.3.3-03','Calculate the constant acceleration for a speed increase from 1 to 7 m/s in 3 s without changing direction.','The acceleration is (7 − 1)/3 = 2 m/s² in the direction of motion.',2,'One mark for velocity change divided by time; one for 2 m/s².')
P('2.3.3-05','Calculate the travelled distance for that uniform speed increase from 1 to 7 m/s during 3 s.','The trapezium area is ½ × (1 + 7) × 3 = 12 m.',2,'One mark for the graph-area relation; one for 12 m.')
A.practical=dict(additional_objective_keys=['2.3.2-06','2.3.2-08','2.3.3-03'],graph='Plot speed vertically against time. The line starts at 0.1 m/s and increases steadily; it is not a distance-time line.',unit='m/s²',meaning='The gradient represents the constant straight-line acceleration over the chosen interval. The area instead represents travelled distance.',method=['Set a model vehicle on a straight track and define a fixed timing start event.','Use suitable position/timing equipment or recorded motion to estimate speed at a sequence of times.','Record the speeds with their corresponding elapsed times while keeping the motion in one direction.','Repeat the run under matched drive and track conditions to assess variation.','Use the speed-time trend to test whether similar speed changes occur during equal time intervals.'],control='Keep the track slope and drive setting matched between repeats; secure the track and keep the run clear.',table='Record elapsed time / s and estimated speed / m s⁻¹, identifying any intermediate distance/time measurements used to obtain speed.',conclusion='Plot speed against time and compare the fitted gradient over the interval. A straight trend supports constant acceleration; do not identify its gradient with the distance.')
if __name__=='__main__':A.build()
